Derek Ray <derek@moot.its.only.a.spamtrap.org> writes:

> Also, it is helpful to maintain a completely clean "vanilla" directory
> next to the directory in which you're modifying files; then from the
> level above both directories, you can use a command like:
> diff -Nurd nethack nh_changed >mypatch.diff

I have the original source tree in a tar.gz packet and for recreating my
combined diff when necessary, I use a script that basically unpacks it in
/tmp after checking that it's not already there, and then does
diff -bNurdX diff_ign.txt nh343orig/ nh343jl/ > /tmp/nh343jl.diff

where nh343orig is a symlink to the original source tree to be created in
/tmp, nh343jl is the directory where I keep my work source, and
diff_ign.txt is a file that contains the exceptions that I don't want in
the diff file (like the binaries and other stuff created in the
compilation process, *~ and #*# for backups created by emacs and work
copies sometimes left behind, and so on).

